<SiteLayout title={mod.title} activeSection="manual" activeSubject="airfield-operations" compactSubjectNav={true}
 breadcrumbs={[{label:'Manual',href:sitePath('manual/')},{label:'Airfield Operations',href:sitePath('manual/airfield-operations/')},{label:mod.title}]}>
 <section class="status-panel" data-module-id={mod.id} data-module-revision={mod.revision}>
  <h2>{mod.maturity}</h2>
  <p>Module revision {mod.revision} · How a military airfield is laid out, marked, signed and lit, how the runway in use, the overhead and the standard pattern work, and how to move safely on the ground. Written for military pilots of every US service: Navy, Air Force, Marine Corps and Army practice comes first. US civil (FAA) standards, which military fields share for most markings, signs, lights and tower rules, are supplementary, and civil towered and non-towered fields are covered as places a military pilot also lands. General radio technique and phraseology are taught in <a href={radioHref}>Radio Procedures</a>. Carrier operations belong to Naval Operations.</p>
  <p>Every figure is interactive. Start with the aerodrome at a glance, then follow an aircraft from the line to the runway, around the pattern and back.</p>
 </section>
 <nav class="contents" aria-labelledby="contents-title"><h2 id="contents-title">In this module</h2>
  <ol>{contents.map(([id,label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol>
 </nav>

 <h2 id="overview">Purpose and learning objectives</h2>
 <p>An airfield is full of standard information written on the ground, on signs and in lights. Pilots who can read it know where they are, where they may go and where they must stop, without needing anyone to tell them.</p>
 <p>By the end you should be able to: work out a runway's number from its direction and tell parallel runways apart; read runway markings, including arresting gear and a displaced threshold; know which side of a holding position marking you must hold on; name each airfield sign by its colours; read runway, taxiway and approach lighting; read the Navy optical landing system's meatball and a PAPI; pick the runway most nearly into the wind and work out the crosswind; describe the overhead from the initial to the break and final, with the Navy and Air Force terms, fly the standard pattern, and know where helicopters fly; make the right calls to the right station at a military field and at civil towered and non-towered airfields; and read tower light-gun signals.</p>
 <p class="note">This is a conceptual reference for military pilots of all services. The military material comes from the Navy's air traffic controller training manual (NAVEDTRA 14342A) and a VT-10 training script, Air Force flying training wings' collision avoidance guides, the Army's airfield operations manual (FM 3-04.300) and NAVAIR's expeditionary airfield program. The shared US standards for markings, signs, lights and tower rules come from the FAA's Aeronautical Information Manual (AIM) and handbooks; military tower controllers work to the same FAA order, 7110.65, with each facility's own procedures added. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nav('9-18')}</a> <a href="#source-fm">{fm('1-19')}</a> Every military field publishes its own course rules, pattern altitudes and procedures, and those always take precedence. Other countries follow ICAO standards that differ in detail.</p>

 <h2 id="aerodrome">The aerodrome at a glance</h2>
 <p>Figure 1 shows a small aerodrome with the pieces this module covers. Select a part to read about it.</p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="aerodrome" aria-labelledby="aero-title">
  <h3 id="aero-title" class="fig-title">Figure 1 — Parts of an aerodrome (plan view)</h3>
  <div class="buttons">
   <button type="button" data-show="runway" aria-pressed="false">Runway</button>
   <button type="button" data-show="numbers" aria-pressed="false">Runway numbers</button>
   <button type="button" data-show="taxiway" aria-pressed="false">Taxiways</button>
   <button type="button" data-show="hold" aria-pressed="false">Holding positions</button>
   <button type="button" data-show="apron" aria-pressed="false">Apron</button>
   <button type="button" data-show="tower" aria-pressed="false">Tower</button>
   <button type="button" data-show="beacon" aria-pressed="false">Beacon</button>
   <button type="button" data-show="circle" aria-pressed="false">Segmented circle</button>
   <button type="button" data-show="sock" aria-pressed="false">Wind cone</button>
  </div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 1, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><AerodromeFigure /></div>
  <ol class="callouts plain">
   <li data-callout="runway"><p><strong>Runway</strong>: the strip used for takeoff and landing. Runway markings are white. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-2.2')}</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout="numbers"><p><strong>Runway numbers</strong>: each end carries the number of its own magnetic direction, so one strip is runway 9 landing east and runway 27 landing west. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-3.2')}</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout="taxiway"><p><strong>Taxiways</strong>: the paths between the runway and the apron. Taxiway markings are yellow; the centerline is a single continuous yellow line. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-2.2, 2-3-4.2')}</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout="hold"><p><strong>Runway holding positions</strong>: four yellow lines across each taxiway entering the runway, with a red runway holding position sign beside them. You stop here unless cleared onto or across the runway. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-5.1, 2-3-8.2')}</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout="apron"><p><strong>Apron (ramp)</strong>: where aircraft park, load and refuel. Approval is needed before moving from it onto the movement area while the tower is operating. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-18')}</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout="tower"><p><strong>Control tower</strong>: where one operates, pilots keep two-way radio contact with it, and a landing clearance from it is always required. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-2')}</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout="beacon"><p><strong>Rotating beacon</strong>: a military field's beacon alternates white and green with a dual-peaked (two quick) white flash between the greens; a lighted civil land airport alternates white and green only. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-1-9')}</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout="circle"><p><strong>Segmented circle</strong>: where installed at an airport without an operating tower, it sits centrally and gathers the wind and traffic pattern indicators; pairs of landing strip indicators show the runway alignment. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-4')}</a></p></li>
   <li data-callout="sock"><p><strong>Wind cone</strong>: its large end points into the wind. This one shows an east wind, so aircraft land on runway 9, toward the east. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-4')}</a></p></li>
  </ol>
 </section>

 <h2 id="numbers">Runway numbers</h2>
 <p>"The runway number is the whole number nearest one-tenth the magnetic azimuth of the centerline of the runway," measured in the direction of approach. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-3.2')}</a> A runway pointing 183° magnetic is runway 18; one pointing 087° is runway 9. For an azimuth ending in 5, such as 185°, the designation could be either 18 or 19. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-6.1')}</a> Each end is numbered for its own direction, so the two ends differ by 18: 9 and 27, 18 and 36. Read literally, the rule gives 0 for a runway pointing within 5° of north; such runways are numbered 36, a convention the AIM paragraph does not state.</p>
 <p>Parallel runways add a letter, read left to right as seen on the approach: L and R for two, L, C and R for three. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-3.2')}</a> The same strip is "left" from one end and "right" from the other.</p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="numbers" aria-labelledby="num-title">
  <h3 id="num-title" class="fig-title">Figure 2 — Runway numbers explorer (plan view, magnetic north up)</h3>
  <div class="controls row">
   <label for="num-az">Runway direction <output for="azimuth"></output><input id="num-az" name="azimuth" type="range" min="0" max="359" step="1" value="87" /></label>
  </div>
  <fieldset class="inline"><legend>Parallel runways</legend>
   <label><input type="radio" name="count" value="1" checked /> One</label>
   <label><input type="radio" name="count" value="2" /> Two</label>
   <label><input type="radio" name="count" value="3" /> Three</label>
  </fieldset>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 2, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 640 440" role="img" aria-label="Compass ring with the runway or runways drawn along the chosen direction and their numbers painted at each end"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. A runway pointing 087° is runway 9; its other end points 267° and is runway 27. Two parallel runways in that direction are 9L and 9R, which are 27R and 27L from the other end.</p>
  <div class="feedback derived" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">The numbers are drawn reading upright to a pilot on the approach to that end. Directions within 5° of north are shown as 36, by the convention described above.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="markings">Runway markings</h2>
 <p>Runway markings are white; markings for taxiways, closed areas and holding positions are yellow, even when a holding position is on a runway. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-2.2')}</a> Which markings a runway carries depends on its type. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('Table 2-3-1')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Runway marking elements by runway type" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>Runway marking elements <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('Table 2-3-1')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Marking</th><th scope="col">Visual runway</th><th scope="col">Nonprecision instrument</th><th scope="col">Precision instrument</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">Designation</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Centerline</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Threshold</th><td>On runways used, or intended to be used, by international commercial transports</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Aiming point</th><td>On runways 4,000 ft (1,200 m) or longer used by jet aircraft</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Touchdown zone</th><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Side stripes</th><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Designation</strong>: the runway number, with any L, C or R. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-3.2')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Centerline</strong>: "a line of uniformly spaced stripes and gaps". <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-3.3')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Threshold</strong>: longitudinal stripes whose number shows the runway width: 4 for 60 ft, 6 for 75 ft, 8 for 100 ft, 12 for 150 ft and 16 for 200 ft. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-3.8, Table 2-3-2')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Aiming point</strong>: two broad white stripes, one each side of the centerline, approximately 1,000 ft from the landing threshold. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-3.4')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Touchdown zone</strong>: groups of one, two and three bars in pairs either side of the centerline, coded to give distance in 500 ft (150 m) increments. Where a runway has them at both ends, pairs within 900 ft of the midpoint are left out. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-3.5')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Side stripes</strong>: continuous white stripes on each side of the runway. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-3.6')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Displaced threshold</strong>: "A ten feet wide white threshold bar is located across the width of the runway at the displaced threshold," with white arrows along the centerline before it. The pavement before a displaced threshold is available for takeoffs in either direction and landings from the opposite direction. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-3.8')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Demarcation bar and chevrons</strong>: a 3 ft wide yellow demarcation bar separates the runway from a blast pad, stopway or taxiway before it; yellow chevrons mark pavement aligned with the runway that is unusable for landing, takeoff and taxiing. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-3.9')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Arresting gear</strong>: military runways carry arresting gear, a pendant cable across the runway that an aircraft's tailhook engages to stop it. Where a cable crosses the runway, a line of 10 ft diameter retroreflective yellow disks is painted across the runway at the cable, and arresting gear signs, large yellow circles on a black background, stand on both sides of the runway beside it. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nav('4-9')}</a> The disks are 30 ft on center across the full runway width. Most emergency arresting gear sits in the overruns, its cable held above the surface by rubber "donuts", but some cables cross the operational runway near its ends. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-14')}</a> Each field publishes where its cables are. The Marine Corps takes the same idea into the field: an expeditionary airfield can be built from AM-2 aluminum matting with M-31 expeditionary arresting gear, its own lighting and terminal guidance, or be as simple as a grass landing zone for helicopters. <a href="#source-eaf">{eaf('EAF')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Closed runway</strong>: yellow crosses at each end of a temporarily closed runway; a permanently closed runway also has crosses every 1,000 ft. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-6.4–2-3-6.5')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="markings" aria-labelledby="mark-title">
  <h3 id="mark-title" class="fig-title">Figure 3 — Runway markings by runway type (plan view)</h3>
  <fieldset class="inline"><legend>Runway type</legend>
   <label><input type="radio" name="rtype" value="visual" /> Visual</label>
   <label><input type="radio" name="rtype" value="nonprecision" /> Nonprecision instrument</label>
   <label><input type="radio" name="rtype" value="precision" checked /> Precision instrument</label>
  </fieldset>
  <div class="controls row">
   <label for="mark-width">Runway width
    <select id="mark-width" name="width"><option value="60">60 ft</option><option value="75">75 ft</option><option value="100">100 ft</option><option value="150" selected>150 ft</option><option value="200">200 ft</option></select>
   </label>
   <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" name="intl" /> Used by international commercial transports</label>
   <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" name="jet" /> 4,000 ft or longer, used by jets</label>
   <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" name="gear" checked /> Arresting gear cable</label>
   <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" name="displaced" /> Displaced threshold</label>
   <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" name="blast" /> Blast pad before the runway</label>
  </div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 3, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 640 320" role="img" aria-label="Runway 9 landing end in plan view with the markings for the chosen runway type, width and options"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. A 150 ft precision instrument runway carries 12 threshold stripes, the designation, a centerline, aiming point stripes about 1,000 ft in, touchdown zone bars and side stripes. An arresting gear cable across it is marked by a line of five 10 ft yellow disks 30 ft apart, with a yellow-circle sign on each side.</p>
  <div class="feedback derived" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">Lengths are to scale along the runway (first 3,600 ft); widths are drawn four times larger so the stripes can be seen. The number of threshold stripes follows the AIM table. The length of each stripe, the centerline dash and gap, the displacement and the bar count in each touchdown zone group (3, aiming point, 2, 2, 1, 1, the usual FAA layout) are not in the AIM text, which gives only the 500 ft coding, so they are unverified. The arresting gear cable is drawn 1,800 ft past the threshold for illustration only; the disks and signs follow NAVEDTRA 14342A and the AIM, and the disk count is the most that fit across the width at 30 ft centers.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="holding">Taxiways and holding positions</h2>
 <p>The taxiway centerline is a single continuous yellow line, 6 to 12 inches wide. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-4.2')}</a> Approaching a runway at larger airports, an <strong>enhanced centerline</strong> adds a parallel line of yellow dashes either side for up to 150 ft before the runway holding position marking. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-4.2')}</a> Taxiway edge markings are a continuous double yellow line where the edge must not be crossed, or a dashed double line where the pavement beyond is usable. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-4.3')}</a></p>
 <p>A <strong>runway holding position marking</strong> is four yellow lines, two solid and two dashed, across the taxiway. "The solid lines are always on the side where the aircraft must hold." <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-5.1')}</a> On a taxiway it shows where aircraft stop when they have no clearance to proceed onto the runway; at an airport with an operating tower, do not cross it without an ATC clearance. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-5.1')}</a> The same marking on a runway is used for land and hold short operations. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-5.1')}</a> An ILS critical area holding position is two solid yellow lines 2 ft apart joined by pairs of solid lines 10 ft apart. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-5.2')}</a> Where the holding position is wider than 200 ft, a red surface-painted holding position sign is added to the left of the centerline on the holding side. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-5.4')}</a></p>
 <p>After landing, exit at the first available taxiway or as ATC instructs, and taxi clear: an aircraft has cleared the runway when all parts of it have crossed the holding position marking. Then hold unless ATC has issued further instructions. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-21')}</a></p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="hold" aria-labelledby="hold-title">
  <h3 id="hold-title" class="fig-title">Figure 4 — Which side do you hold on? (plan view)</h3>
  <fieldset class="inline"><legend>You are</legend>
   <label><input type="radio" name="way" value="enter" checked /> Taxiing toward the runway</label>
   <label><input type="radio" name="way" value="exit" /> Exiting after landing</label>
  </fieldset>
  <div class="controls row">
   <label for="hold-pos">Aircraft nose position <output for="pos"></output><input id="hold-pos" name="pos" type="range" min="-140" max="140" step="5" value="-40" /></label>
  </div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 4, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 640 440" role="img" aria-label="Taxiway meeting a runway with the holding position marking, enhanced centerline, location and holding position signs, and an aircraft at the chosen position"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. Taxiing toward the runway, hold on the side with the two solid lines. Exiting, keep going until every part of the aircraft has crossed the marking.</p>
  <div class="feedback derived" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">The aircraft is 30 ft long. Distances are not to scale.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="signs">Airport signs</h2>
 <p>Signs are coded by colour as well as by what they say. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-7–2-3-13')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Mandatory instruction signs</strong>: red background, white inscription. They mark the entrance to a runway or critical area, or an area aircraft may not enter. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-8')}</a> A <em>runway holding position sign</em> carries the runway's designation; "15-33" means runway 15's threshold is to the left and runway 33's to the right. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-8.2')}</a> An <em>ILS critical area holding position sign</em> reads "ILS". <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-8.3')}</a> A <em>no entry sign</em> prohibits aircraft from entering an area, such as a one-way taxiway or a road that could be mistaken for one. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-8.4')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Location signs</strong>: black background, yellow inscription and yellow border. A taxiway location sign tells you which taxiway you are on, alone or with direction or holding position signs. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-9')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Direction signs</strong>: yellow background, black inscription, with arrows to the taxiways leading off an intersection. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-10')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Destination signs</strong>: yellow background, black inscription, with an arrow toward a destination such as a runway or the ramp. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-11')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Information signs</strong>: yellow background, black inscription. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-12')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Runway distance remaining signs</strong>: black background, white numeral: the thousands of feet of landing runway remaining. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-3-13')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="signs" aria-labelledby="signs-title">
  <h3 id="signs-title" class="fig-title">Figure 5 — Sign decoder</h3>
  <p>Select a sign to read it.</p>
  <div class="sign-gallery buttons" data-gallery></div>
  <p class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="sign-quiz" data-quiz>
   <h4>Quiz: what kind of sign is this?</h4>
   <div class="sign-shown" data-sign role="img"></div>
   <div class="buttons" data-choices></div>
   <p class="feedback" data-quiz-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
   <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-new>Another sign</button></div>
  </div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. Red with white text: mandatory instruction. Black with yellow text and border: location. Yellow with black text: direction, destination or information. Black with a white number: runway distance remaining.</p>
  <p class="fig-caption">Signs are drawn from the AIM's colour descriptions; their proportions and lettering are illustrative. The taxiway and destination names are examples.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="lighting">Airfield lighting</h2>
 <p>At night the same layout is written in coloured lights. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-1-4–2-1-10')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Runway edge lights</strong> are white. On instrument runways, yellow replaces white on the last 2,000 ft or half the runway length, whichever is less, to form a caution zone for landings. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-1-4.2')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Runway end lights</strong> emit red toward the runway to mark its end for departing aircraft, and green outward from the runway end to mark the threshold for landing aircraft. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-1-4.3')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Runway centerline lights</strong> are white until the last 3,000 ft, then alternate red and white for the next 2,000 ft; the last 1,000 ft are all red. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-1-5.1')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Touchdown zone lights</strong> are steady white lights starting 100 ft beyond the landing threshold and extending to 3,000 ft beyond it or to the runway midpoint, whichever is less. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-1-5.2')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Runway end identifier lights</strong> (REIL) are a pair of synchronized flashing lights, one each side of the threshold. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-1-3')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Taxiway edge lights</strong> are blue; <strong>taxiway centerline lights</strong> are steady green. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-1-10')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Rotating beacons</strong> flash 24 to 30 times a minute at airports. Military airport beacons alternate white and green and are told apart by dual-peaked (two quick) white flashes between the green flashes; a lighted civil land airport alternates white and green only. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-1-9')}</a> In Class B, C, D and E surface areas, a beacon operating by day often means the ground visibility is less than 3 miles and/or the <Term>ceiling</Term> is less than 1,000 ft, but pilots should not rely solely on it to tell whether conditions are <Term>IFR</Term> or <Term>VFR</Term>. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-1-9')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="lighting" aria-labelledby="light-title">
