 <h2 id="glidepath">Visual glide slope indicators</h2>
 <p>Most military runways, like civil ones, use the PAPI or VASI below. Naval air stations whose runways are used for field carrier landing practice (FCLP) also have an <strong>optical landing system</strong> (OLS), often the Improved Fresnel Lens OLS (IFLOLS), the same kind of system pilots fly to the carrier. It is installed <Term>abeam</Term> the touchdown point on the left side of the runway, and it gives glide slope information only: it does not show alignment with the centerline. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nav('4-25')}</a> Its parts are: <a href="#source-navedtra">{nav('4-25')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li>the <strong>meatball</strong>, or <strong><Term id="Ball">ball</Term></strong>: a yellow light in the lens box, read against the datum lights to see where you are on the glide slope;</li>
  <li>the <strong>datum lights</strong>: a horizontal bar of green lights each side of the lens box, the reference the ball is judged against;</li>
  <li>the <strong>wave-off lights</strong>: flashing red lights, worked by the <Term id="Landing Signal Officer (LSO)">landing signal officer (LSO)</Term> during FCLP, which tell the pilot to <Term>go around</Term>;</li>
  <li>the <strong>cut lights</strong>: green lights above the lens box, which the LSO uses to acknowledge control of an aircraft without a radio (NORDO).</li>
 </ul>
 <p>"The ball rises above the datum lights as the pilot rises above the glide slope," and slides off the top of the lens box when the aircraft is more than 3/4° above it. Well below, the ball slides off the bottom; before then, a red lens in the bottom cell of the box turns the ball red, the low-ball warning. The glide slope angle is whatever the lens is set to. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nav('5-52')}</a> Flying the ball to touchdown, and the LSO's calls, are taught with carrier landings in Naval Operations.</p>
 <p>A visual approach slope indicator (VASI) works by colour: each light unit projects a beam with a white segment in its upper part and a red segment in its lower part. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-1-2.1')}</a> Most VASIs have two bars, near and far, giving one visual glide path normally set at 3°. Its safe obstruction clearance extends within ±10° of the extended runway centerline and out to 4 NM from the threshold. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-1-2.1')}</a> A three-bar VASI adds a second, upper glide path, normally 1/4° higher, for high-cockpit aircraft. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-1-2.1')}</a></p>
 <p>The <strong>precision approach path indicator</strong> (PAPI), found at Navy and civil fields alike, uses the same red and white principle in a single row of two or four light units, normally on the left side of the runway. It typically gives safe obstruction clearance within ±10° of the extended centerline and to 3.4 NM from the threshold, and can be seen from about 5 miles by day and up to 20 miles at night. Do not start a descent using the PAPI until the aircraft is visually aligned with the runway. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-1-2.2')}</a></p>
 <p>A <strong>tri-color</strong> system is a single light: red below the glide path, amber above it and green on it. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-1-2.3')}</a> A <strong>pulsating</strong> system shows steady white on the path, steady red slightly below, pulsing red further below and pulsing white above, pulsing faster the further off the path you are. On some systems the on-path indication is alternating red and white instead. <a href="#source-aim2">{aim2('2-1-2.4')}</a></p>
