 <h2 id="wind">Wind and the runway in use</h2>
 <p>Military tower controllers, here as the Navy's manual puts it, select the runway most nearly aligned with the wind when it is 5 knots or more, and the calm-wind runway when it is less than 5 knots, unless a local runway use program, such as noise abatement, says otherwise. They issue the wind direction and speed with the runway, and call the wind "calm" only below 3 knots. Another runway can be used if it is operationally advantageous or the pilot asks for it, and the controller then states the runway number in the clearance. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nav('9-8–9-9')}</a> Civil towers apply the same 5 knot rule. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-6.2')}</a></p>
 <p>Without a tower, the airport may show the wind and landing direction on the ground. "The large end of the wind cone/wind sock points into the wind as does the large end (cross bar) of the wind tee." The small end of a <strong>tetrahedron</strong> points in the direction of landing; pilots should not use it as a wind indicator, and tower instructions supersede it. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-4')}</a> A <strong>segmented circle</strong>, if installed, gathers these with landing strip and traffic pattern indicators. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-4')}</a></p>
 <p>The part of the wind blowing across the runway is the <strong>crosswind component</strong>; the part along it is the head- or tailwind. For a wind of speed V at an angle θ to the runway, crosswind = V sin θ and headwind = V cos θ. A 20 kt wind 30° off the runway gives 10 kt of crosswind and about 17 kt of headwind.</p>
