 <h2 id="pattern">The traffic pattern</h2>
 <p>Military airfields run two kinds of pattern: a <strong>standard</strong> (rectangular, or VFR) pattern and the <strong><Term id="Overhead maneuver">overhead</Term></strong>. In the Navy's words, every airfield has both; traffic normally flows left, with right traffic when required; the legs are upwind, crosswind, downwind, base and final; and a downwind entry joins at 45°. Altitudes vary with the station and its aircraft, so they are published locally. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nav('9-11–9-12')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Air Force</strong>: at Columbus AFB, a pilot training base, the VFR pattern is like the civil one except for the entry, where the guide singles out the civil 45° entry to downwind as the difference; aircraft turn crosswind about 1 NM past the runway. Each aircraft type there has its own runway side, pattern area and altitude band, and the T-6 pattern is run by a <strong>runway supervisory unit</strong> (RSU) on its own frequency. <a href="#source-cbm">{cbm('Military traffic pattern')}</a> Single-engine jets and trainers also fly <strong>simulated flameout</strong> (SFO) or emergency landing patterns, high-altitude glides to the runway with very high descent rates. <a href="#source-jbsa">{jbsa('Kelly Field; ELP')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Helicopters</strong>, the Army's and the Marine Corps' above all, may fly a pattern like the fixed-wing one but lower, at 500 ft AGL, and closer to the runway, or on the opposite side where airspeed or practice autorotations call for it, if local policy allows; landings away from the runway stay out of the fixed-wing flow. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-3')}</a> Army fields add helipads, hoverpoints and rotary-wing landing lanes. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('3-9, App. C')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p>The leg definitions and numbers below are the FAA's, which military towers also work to.</p>
 <p>The <Term>traffic pattern</Term> organises arrivals and departures around a runway. It is a rectangle with turns to the left unless the airport shows right turns. <a href="#source-afh">{afh('p. 8-1')}</a> <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-3')}</a> Its legs are: <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-3')}</a> <a href="#source-pcg">{pcg('Traffic pattern')}</a></p>
 <ol>
  <li><strong>Departure leg</strong>: straight ahead along the extended runway centerline after takeoff, until at least 1/2 mile beyond the runway end and within 300 ft of pattern altitude. An <strong><Term>upwind leg</Term></strong> extends it where needed for separation, spacing or sequencing.</li>
  <li><strong><Term>Crosswind leg</Term></strong>: at right angles to the landing runway, off its takeoff end. Turn onto it beyond the departure end of the runway, within 300 ft of pattern altitude.</li>
  <li><strong><Term>Downwind leg</Term></strong>: parallel to the landing runway, in the opposite direction to landing.</li>
  <li><strong><Term>Base leg</Term></strong>: at right angles to the landing runway, off its approach end, from the downwind leg to the extended centerline.</li>
  <li><strong><Term>Final approach</Term></strong>: along the extended runway centerline, in the direction of landing, from the base leg to the runway. Complete the turn to final at least 1/4 mile from the runway.</li>
 </ol>
 <p><strong>Altitude.</strong> At a Navy field, fly the altitudes in the field's course rules. At a civil field, propeller-driven aircraft enter the pattern at 1,000 ft above ground level (AGL); large and turbine-powered aircraft, a military jet included, at not less than 1,500 ft AGL or 500 ft above the established pattern altitude. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-3')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Spacing and turns.</strong> The downwind leg is flown approximately 1/2 to 1 mile out from the landing runway. The turn to base starts at a point approximately 45° from the approach end of the runway. <a href="#source-afh">{afh('p. 8-3')}</a> When aircraft are approaching to land, the one at the lower altitude has the right-of-way, but it should not use that to cut in front of or overtake an aircraft already on final. <a href="#source-afh">{afh('p. 8-4')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Entering.</strong> "Entry to the downwind leg should be at a 45-degree angle abeam the midpoint of the runway to be used for landing." <a href="#source-ac">{ac('11.3')}</a> From the upwind side, the Airplane Flying Handbook describes crossing over midfield at least 500 ft above pattern altitude, descending to pattern altitude about 2 miles out, and turning to enter at 45°. <a href="#source-afh">{afh('p. 8-4')}</a> The FAA discourages VFR straight-in approaches because they raise the risk of a midair collision. <a href="#source-ac">{ac('9.11.1')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Leaving.</strong> Continue straight out or exit with a 45° turn, beyond the departure end of the runway after reaching pattern altitude. <a href="#source-ac">{ac('11.8')}</a> <a href="#source-afh">{afh('p. 8-4')}</a></p>
