 <h2 id="overhead">The overhead maneuver</h2>
 <p>"The overhead approach pattern is a series of predetermined maneuvers prescribed for VFR arrival of military aircraft," often flown in formation. It lets high-performance aircraft enter the terminal area alongside slower aircraft flying the standard pattern. The Navy's manual lists its components: <a href="#source-navedtra">{nav('9-13')}</a></p>
 <ol>
  <li><strong><Term id="Initial">Initial approach</Term></strong>: a line of flight along the extended centerline of the landing runway, 3 to 5 miles long, normally flown 500 ft above the standard traffic pattern altitude.</li>
  <li><strong><Term>Break point</Term></strong>: normally just above the landing threshold, where the aircraft turns 180°, left or right with the traffic flow, onto the downwind leg.</li>
  <li><strong><Term id="The 180">"The 180"</Term></strong>: after the downwind leg, a second 180° turn puts the aircraft on final approach; it stays at traffic pattern altitude until that turn starts. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nav('9-14')}</a> The training script's landing call names the pilot's position as abeam, the 180, the 90 or final, then reports the gear. <a href="#source-hollywood">{hw('33–34')}</a> This module takes the 180 as the start of that turn and the 90 as its midpoint, an interpretation the sources do not spell out.</li>
  <li><strong>Final approach</strong>: along the extended centerline to the runway.</li>
 </ol>
 <p>The Air Force flies the same maneuver with its own words. At Columbus AFB the aircraft flies to <strong>initial</strong>, about 4 NM out on the runway centerline, crosses the runway at 1,000 to 1,500 ft AGL and "breaks" in a hard turn to lose airspeed onto the <strong>inside downwind</strong>, within 1.5 NM of the runway. After a <Term>touch-and-go</Term> or <Term>low approach</Term> a pilot can <strong>pull closed</strong>: climb and turn straight back onto the inside downwind instead of flying the whole pattern again. <a href="#source-cbm">{cbm('Military traffic pattern')}</a> Altitudes are local: San Antonio's F-16s fly their overhead at 2,700 ft MSL. <a href="#source-jbsa">{jbsa('Kelly Field')}</a></p>
 <p>The FAA's definitions say the same in more detail, and are what a civil field with an overhead uses. The tower uses an overhead maneuver where aircraft have an operational need for it, often at military fields. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('3-10-12')}</a> The Pilot/Controller Glossary defines it as "A series of predetermined maneuvers prescribed for aircraft (often in formation)" for entry into the visual flight rules (VFR) traffic pattern and to proceed to a landing. It is not an instrument (IFR) approach procedure: an aircraft flying it is VFR, and its IFR flight plan is cancelled at the "initial point" on the initial approach. <a href="#source-pcg">{pcg('Overhead maneuver')}</a> The pattern usually specifies: <a href="#source-pcg">{pcg('Overhead maneuver')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li>the radio contact required of the pilot, and the speed to be maintained;</li>
  <li>an initial approach 3 to 5 miles in length;</li>
  <li>an elliptical pattern consisting of two 180° turns;</li>
  <li>a break point at which the first 180° turn is started, and the direction of turns;</li>
  <li>an altitude at least 500 ft above the conventional pattern;</li>
  <li>a roll-out on final approach not less than 1/4 mile from the landing threshold and not less than 300 ft above the ground.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>Overhead patterns are developed at airports where aircraft have an operational need for them, and a standard overhead pattern does not rule out the conventional rectangular pattern. If the break point is not standard, the tower names it, for example "break at midfield". <a href="#source-atc">{atc('3-10-12')}</a></p>
