 <h2 id="talk">Talking to the airfield</h2>
 <p>Most of the above is the same at every airfield. What changes is who you talk to. At a military field you usually work tower and approach on UHF, which many civil aircraft cannot hear, <a href="#source-jbsa">{jbsa('2')}</a> fly the field's published course rules to the initial, and may talk to your own <Term>squadron</Term> or a runway supervisory unit on frequencies of their own. At a civil towered airfield the same tower rules apply in civil wording. At an airfield without a tower, there is nobody to clear you: every pilot broadcasts on one shared frequency and sorts out the traffic by announcing where they are and what they will do. This section covers who to call and when. How to make a call, say numbers and read back a clearance is taught in <a href={radioHref}>Radio Procedures</a>, along with what to do when the radio fails.</p>
 <h3>Military fields</h3>
 <p>The calls differ by service and base, so this section and Figure 11 use one worked example: the Navy VT-10 training script, a complete T-6A <Term>sortie</Term> of radio calls from a naval air station. <a href="#source-hollywood">{hw('19–35')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Before taxi</strong>: copy the <Term>ATIS</Term> (preset UHF 1), tell the squadron's base frequency which aircraft you have and that you are taxiing, then ask ground for taxi with the ATIS letter. An IFR departure copies its clearance from clearance delivery first. <a href="#source-hollywood">{hw('19–21')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Departure</strong>: a last "going flying" call to base, then tower for takeoff, then check in with departure climbing, giving the altitude you are passing and the one you are climbing to. <a href="#source-hollywood">{hw('25–27')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Return</strong>: ask approach for the course rules with the ATIS letter, report the course rules point in sight, and switch to tower when told, by preset channel ("contact tower Button 4"). Call the tower at the point "for the break"; report the numbers; the tower approves the break. At the 180 report your position, "gear" and "stop", and get your landing clearance. <a href="#source-hollywood">{hw('31–34')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>After landing</strong>: stay with tower until clear of the runway, taxi back to the line with ground, and tell base you are "in and up". <a href="#source-hollywood">{hw('34–35')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p>If you are not sure your landing gear is down and locked, tell the tower. It can call you by for a low pass in front of the tower and tell you what it sees; if any doubt remains, it alerts the crash and rescue crews and you make a precautionary landing, staying on the runway until ground personnel have checked the gear. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nav('9-6')}</a> An aircraft in distress has the right of way over all other traffic. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nav('9-9')}</a></p>
 <p>Other services' differences to know: an Air Force training base runs its trainer pattern through a runway supervisory unit on its own frequency, alongside the tower. <a href="#source-cbm">{cbm('T-6')}</a> Air Force trainers practicing at non-towered civil fields monitor the CTAF, make position reports and normally fly low approaches. <a href="#source-jbsa">{jbsa('ELP')}</a> Helicopters move on the airfield by taxi, <Term>hover taxi</Term> or <Term>air taxi</Term>: air taxi is preferred where conditions allow, below 100 ft AGL unless cleared otherwise, and a helicopter asks before hovering above 25 ft AGL. They can be cleared to take off and land away from the runway, and ATC will not issue a downwind takeoff with more than 5 kt of tailwind unless the pilot asks. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-17')}</a></p>
 <h3>Civil towered airfields</h3>
 <p>Inside the surface area of a towered airfield you must keep two-way radio contact with the tower unless it authorizes otherwise. Make the initial call about 15 miles out. The tower then issues the instructions that shape your path, such as which leg of the pattern to join and what to report, and a clearance from the tower is always required before landing. Not every towered airfield sits in Class D airspace. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-2')}</a> Listen to the recorded airport information (ATIS) before the first call so you can say you have it; <a href={radioHref}>Radio Procedures</a> shows how.</p>
 <p>The airfield has more than one controller, each on its own frequency. <strong>Ground control</strong> handles the taxiways and <strong>tower (local control)</strong> handles the runway and the air around it. Departing, call ground control or clearance delivery before starting engines, stay on that frequency through taxi and runup, and change to the tower only when ready to request takeoff clearance. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-14')}</a> When departure delays are expected to exceed 15 minutes, gate hold procedures apply, and the call before engine start is how you get your start time. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-15')}</a> State your position on the airport whenever you ask for taxi, and read back taxi instructions; ATC must get a readback of every runway hold short instruction. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-18')}</a> After landing, stay with the tower until it tells you to contact ground, then change immediately and get a taxi clearance. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-14, 4-3-21')}</a></p>
 <h3>Non-towered airfields</h3>
 <p>Where there is no operating tower, traffic advisories happen on the <strong>common traffic advisory frequency</strong> (CTAF). Where the airfield has no tower, CTAF or UNICOM published, use the MULTICOM frequency, 122.9 MHz. <a href="#source-ac">{ac('6.3, 9.6')}</a> All traffic, VFR or IFR, should at least monitor the CTAF. <a href="#source-ac">{ac('9.5')}</a> A UNICOM station, where there is one, may give you the weather and wind on request, but its operator is not required to talk to you. <a href="#source-ac">{ac('9.3')}</a></p>
 <p>The calls are <strong>self-announcements</strong>: call sign, aircraft type, position, altitude and intentions. <a href="#source-ac">{ac('9.8, 9.8.1')}</a> Start and end each one with the airport's published name, not the name of the town nearby, and use the runway number rather than "the active runway". <a href="#source-ac">{ac('9.8.1, 10.1')}</a> Avoid "to" and "for", which can be heard as numbers. <a href="#source-ac">{ac('10.3')}</a> Report your position, not your guess at the landing order. <a href="#source-ac">{ac('10.1')}</a> "Any traffic in the area, please advise" is not a recognized call; traffic that may conflict should answer an announcement without being asked. <a href="#source-ac">{ac('9.8.1')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Inbound</strong>: about 10 miles out, monitor the CTAF and announce your position, altitude and intentions; <a href="#source-ac">{ac('10.4.1')}</a> then announce entering the downwind leg, and each leg after it. <a href="#source-ac">{ac('9.11.2')}</a> A pilot flying a straight-in approach announces between about 8 and 10 miles out. <a href="#source-ac">{ac('9.11.1')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Outbound</strong>: monitor the CTAF from engine start, through taxi and after departure. <a href="#source-ac">{ac('10.4.2')}</a> Announce your intentions before taxi, and before entering the runway announce the runway and your direction of flight. <a href="#source-ac">{ac('9.5, 10.4.3')}</a> An IFR departure keeps monitoring the CTAF until it is in contact with ATC, and an IFR release is not authorization to take off. <a href="#source-ac">{ac('9.5, 10.4.3')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p>A <strong>part-time tower</strong> changes the rules with the clock. When the tower is closed, or operates only part of the day, use the published CTAF to self-announce within 10 miles. <a href="#source-ac">{ac('9.8')}</a> Check the field's published hours and frequencies before you go.</p>
