  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. Navy field, arriving: ATIS; approach for the course rules; the course rules point in sight, then tower by preset channel; the point "for the break"; the numbers, break approved; the 180, gear, stop, cleared to land; stay with tower until clear; ground to taxi to the line. Navy field, departing: ATIS; base, taxiing outbound; ground for taxi; tower for takeoff; departure, climbing. Civil towered, arriving: ATIS; tower about 15 miles out; report as instructed until cleared to land; stay with tower until told to contact ground; ground for taxi. Civil towered, departing: ATIS; ground before engine start; read back the taxi instructions; tower when ready for takeoff; stay with tower until it approves a frequency change. Non-towered, arriving: UNICOM or automated weather; self-announce on the CTAF about 10 miles out, entering downwind, on base, on final and clear of the runway. Non-towered, departing: monitor the CTAF from engine start; announce before taxi and before entering the runway; keep monitoring after departure.</p>
  <p class="fig-caption">Cedar is a made-up airfield with runway 27 in use and left traffic. The Navy field calls follow the VT-10 script's formats, moved to Cedar with the call sign KATT 11; preset channel numbers are the script's. The civil calls are examples in the formats the AIM and AC 90-66C give, with numbers spelled out as you would say them. Controller replies are typical, not quoted. Real controllers will give different instructions, and every airfield publishes its own frequencies and procedures. Not to scale.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="ground">Moving on the ground</h2>
 <p>At an airport with an operating tower, approval is needed before moving an aircraft or vehicle onto the movement area, and a clearance is needed before taxiing on a runway, taking off, landing or crossing any runway. ATC issues an explicit clearance for every runway crossing, and a taxi instruction to the departure runway is not a clearance to enter or cross it. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-18')}</a> Pilots unfamiliar with an airport can ask for progressive taxi instructions, and should remember that jet blast, prop wash and rotor wash can damage aircraft and equipment close behind. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-18')}</a> Who you call for taxi, and when, is in <a href="#talk">Talking to the airfield</a>.</p>
 <p>Towers control aircraft, vehicles and people without radios, or whose radio contact cannot be established, with a light gun: an intense narrow beam of a selected colour. Light signals can only approve or disapprove what the pilot is expected to do, and a pilot may not be looking at the tower when a signal is sent. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-13')}</a> Navy towers use the same signals for aircraft. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nav('9-10')}</a> At an FCLP runway the LSO has the lens's own lights as well: flashing red wave-off lights, and green cut lights to acknowledge an aircraft without a radio. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nav('4-25')}</a></p>
