 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <details><summary>Inbound to a Navy field on the initial. Where do you break, and how high are you?</summary><p>Normally just above the landing threshold, turning 180° onto downwind with the traffic flow. The initial is normally flown 500 ft above the standard pattern altitude.</p></details>
 <details><summary>At an Air Force base you finish a touch-and-go and want another landing. What can you ask for?</summary><p>To pull closed: climb and turn straight onto the inside downwind instead of flying out to initial again, if the pattern allows.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You fly a helicopter into a towered field with fixed-wing traffic. Where is your pattern?</summary><p>Lower and closer: about 500 ft AGL and closer to the runway, or on the other side where local policy allows, keeping clear of the fixed-wing flow.</p></details>
 <details><summary>A line of yellow disks crosses the runway ahead, with a yellow circle on a black sign at each side. What is it?</summary><p>An arresting gear pendant cable.</p></details>
 <details><summary>The meatball is above the green datum lights. Are you high or low? And if it turns red?</summary><p>High: the ball rises above the datum as you rise above the glide slope. A red ball is the low-ball warning in the bottom cell of the lens: you are well low.</p></details>
 <details><summary>The LSO flashes the wave-off lights at you on an FCLP pass. What do you do?</summary><p>Go around. The flashing red wave-off lights tell the pilot to execute a missed approach.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You are not sure your gear is down and locked. What happens?</summary><p>Tell the tower. It can have you make a low pass for a visual check; if doubt remains, crash and rescue are alerted and you make a precautionary landing, staying on the runway until ground personnel have checked the gear.</p></details>
 <details><summary>A runway's centerline points 004° magnetic. What is it numbered, and what is its other end?</summary><p>Runway 36, since 0.4 rounds to 0 and north is written 36. The other end points 184°: runway 18.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You are approaching three parallel runways numbered 27. Which one is on your left?</summary><p>27L. Seen from the other end it is 9R.</p></details>
 <details><summary>A 100 ft wide runway: how many threshold stripes?</summary><p>Eight.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You are taxiing toward a runway and see two solid and two dashed yellow lines. Which side must you stop on?</summary><p>The side with the solid lines, which is yours as you approach from the taxiway. Do not cross without a clearance.</p></details>
 <details><summary>After landing you stop with your tail just over the dashed lines. Are you clear of the runway?</summary><p>No. All parts of the aircraft must cross the holding position marking, the solid lines included, before you are clear.</p></details>
 <details><summary>The sign beside you is black with a yellow "B" and a yellow border. What does it tell you?</summary><p>You are on taxiway B. It is a location sign.</p></details>
 <details><summary>The edge lights ahead have turned yellow. What does that mean?</summary><p>You are in the caution zone of an instrument runway: the last 2,000 ft, or half the runway if that is less.</p></details>
 <details><summary>On approach, the PAPI shows three red and one white. What do you do?</summary><p>You are slightly low. The usual technique, which is teaching guidance rather than AIM text, is to reduce your rate of descent until you regain two white and two red.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Wind 240° at 15 kt, runways 27 and 18. Which is in use, and how much crosswind?</summary><p>Runway 27, 30° off the wind (runway 18 is 60° off). Crosswind 15 × sin 30° = 7.5 kt from the left.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Where and how do you join the pattern?</summary><p>At pattern altitude, at 45° to the downwind leg, abeam the runway midpoint.</p></details>
 <details><summary>An overhead pattern's conventional pattern altitude is 1,000 ft AGL. What is the lowest altitude for the overhead?</summary><p>1,500 ft AGL: at least 500 ft above the conventional pattern.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You are taxiing without a radio and the tower shows you a flashing green. What does it mean?</summary><p>Cleared for taxi. A steady green would clear you for takeoff.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You are 15 miles from a towered airfield. Who do you call first, and what do you have ready?</summary><p>At a civil field, the tower, about 15 miles out, having already listened to the ATIS so you can say you have it. Inside the surface area you keep two-way contact with the tower. At a Navy field you would normally already be with approach on the course rules, which hands you to the tower.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You have just landed at a towered field and cleared the runway. When do you change to ground?</summary><p>Only when the tower tells you to. Then change immediately and ask ground for taxi, stating where you are.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Ten miles from a non-towered field. What do you say, and to whom?</summary><p>Self-announce on the CTAF to the other traffic: airport name, call sign and type, position, altitude and intentions, ending with the airport name again. Nobody will clear you to land.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Departing a non-towered field: when do you talk?</summary><p>Monitor the CTAF from engine start. Announce before taxi, and before entering the runway announce the runway and your direction of flight. Keep monitoring after departure.</p></details>

 <h2 id="sources">Sources and applicability</h2>
 <h3>Military sources</h3>
 <p id="source-navedtra"><strong>[1] Naval Education and Training Command, NAVEDTRA 14342A, Air Traffic Controller</strong> (Navy rate training manual), as published online on <a href="https://navyaviation.tpub.com/14342A/">navyaviation.tpub.com</a>. Inspected 25 October 2026, printed pages 4-9 (arresting gear), 4-25 (optical landing system), 5-52 (IFLOLS), 9-6 (landing gear checks), 9-8 to 9-9 (runway use, priority), 9-10 (light gun), 9-11 to 9-14 (traffic patterns, overhead) and 9-18; page links are in the review evidence. A US Navy training manual, reproduced by a third-party site; the edition date is not shown on the pages read.</p>
 <p id="source-hollywood"><strong>[2] Training Squadron TEN (VT-10), "T-6A Checklist Study Guide ('Hollywood Script')", enclosure (1) to TRARONTENNOTE 3710, May 2022.</strong> <a href="https://www.cnatra.navy.mil/tw6/vt10/assets/docs/training/hollywood-script.pdf">Read the script (cnatra.navy.mil)</a>. Inspected 25 October 2026: radio calls pp. 19–35. Used for the format of Navy field calls; the review gate cannot reach this host, so claims resting on it are unverified.</p>
 <p id="source-cbm"><strong>[3] 14th Flying Training Wing Safety Office, Columbus Air Force Base, "Columbus Collision Avoidance: A guide to Columbus Air Force Base and the surrounding area", October 2013.</strong> <a href="https://flytupelo.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/12/MACA-Booklet.pdf">PDF, as hosted by Tupelo Regional Airport</a>. Inspected 25 October 2026: "Aircraft at Columbus" and "Military Traffic Pattern" sections. An Air Force wing's guide for civil pilots; dated, so treat its numbers as an example.</p>
 <p id="source-jbsa"><strong>[4] Joint Base San Antonio, "Midair Collision Avoidance (MACA) Handbook".</strong> <a href="https://www.jbsa.mil/Portals/102/Documents/Safety/JBSA-MACA_Pamphlet_2024.pdf">PDF (jbsa.mil)</a>. Inspected 25 October 2026: p. 2, the JBSA-Kelly Field and emergency landing pattern (ELP) sections.</p>
 <p id="source-fm"><strong>[5] Headquarters, Department of the Army, FM 3-04.300, Airfield and Flight Operations Procedures, 12 August 2008 with Change 1, 21 April 2010.</strong> <a href="https://irp.fas.org/doddir/army/fm3-04-300.pdf">PDF (irp.fas.org)</a>. Inspected 25 October 2026: ¶1-19, 3-9 and Appendix C (the extractor did not reach the table text).</p>
 <p id="source-eaf"><strong>[6] Naval Air Systems Command, "Expeditionary Airfields".</strong> <a href="https://www.navair.navy.mil/product/expeditionary-airfields-0">navair.navy.mil</a>. Inspected 25 October 2026.</p>
 <h3>Supplementary: FAA sources</h3>
 <p id="source-aim2"><strong>[7] Federal Aviation Administration, Aeronautical Information Manual (AIM), Chapter 2, Aeronautical Lighting and Other Airport Visual Aids:</strong> <a href="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap2_section_1.html">Section 1, Airport Lighting Aids</a> and <a href="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap2_section_3.html">Section 3, Airport Marking Aids and Signs</a> (faa.gov, current online edition). Inspected 25 October 2026: ¶2-1-2 to 2-1-10; ¶2-3-2 to 2-3-14 with Tables 2-3-1 and 2-3-2. US government work.</p>
 <p id="source-aim4"><strong>[8] FAA, AIM, Chapter 4, Section 3, Airport Operations:</strong> <a href="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap4_section_3.html">faa.gov</a>. Inspected 25 October 2026: ¶4-3-2, 4-3-3, 4-3-4, 4-3-6, 4-3-13 (light signal table), 4-3-14, 4-3-15, 4-3-17, 4-3-18 and 4-3-21.</p>
 <p id="source-pcg"><strong>[9] FAA, Pilot/Controller Glossary:</strong> <a href="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/pcg_html/glossary-o.html">"Overhead maneuver" (O)</a>; <a href="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/pcg_html/glossary-t.html">"Threshold", "Traffic pattern" and "Tetrahedron" (T)</a> (faa.gov). Inspected 25 October 2026.</p>
 <p id="source-afh"><strong>[10] FAA, Airplane Flying Handbook (FAA-H-8083-3C), Chapter 8, Airport Traffic Patterns:</strong> <a href="https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/airplane_handbook/09_afh_ch8.pdf">PDF (faa.gov)</a>, pp. 8-1 to 8-4. Inspected 25 October 2026.</p>
 <p id="source-ac"><strong>[11] FAA, Advisory Circular AC 90-66C, Non-Towered Airport Flight Operations, 6 June 2023:</strong> <a href="https://www.faa.gov/documentlibrary/media/advisory_circular/ac_90-66c.pdf">PDF (faa.gov)</a>, ¶9.3, 9.5, 9.6, 9.8, 9.8.1, 9.11.1, 9.11.2, 10.1, 10.3, 10.4, 11.3 and 11.8, and Appendix B. Inspected 25 October 2026.</p>
 <p id="source-atc"><strong>[12] FAA Order JO 7110.65, Air Traffic Control, ¶3-10-12, Overhead Maneuver:</strong> <a href="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/atc_html/chap3_section_10.html">faa.gov</a>. Inspected 25 October 2026.</p>
 <p>Page-specific excerpts are recorded in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/airfield-ops-faa-evidence.md">airfield evidence (Navy and FAA)</a>.</p>
 <p>Figures 1–12 are original line drawings, not reproductions of any Navy or FAA figure, and are not to scale except where a caption says so. The wind, glide slope and overhead figures use the simple models described with them.</p>
 <p class="note">Applicability: military pilots of all US services first, with Navy, Air Force, Marine Corps and Army airfield practice from the military sources above, and the US (FAA) standards military and civil fields share as the supplementary reference, as published online on the inspection date. Civil towered and non-towered procedures are included for the civil fields a military pilot also uses. Every air station's course rules and published procedures take precedence, and other services, other nations and ICAO states differ in detail. This revision is Drafted pending independent source-backed review.</p>
